---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { CONDUCT_EMAIL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="conduct"
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="How to report a problem, what happens next, and what we act on."
>
  <p>
    This page starts with reporting, because that is the only part anyone needs at the moment
    something goes wrong. The rules are below it.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting something</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Mail <a href={`mailto:${CONDUCT_EMAIL}`}>{CONDUCT_EMAIL}</a>.</strong> That reaches Ryan
      Grippeling, the organiser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Or find him on the night.</strong> He introduces himself at the opening and stays visible,
      so you do not have to work out who to ask for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Is it about him?</strong> Then that address is the wrong route. Tell the venue host, or
      anyone you trust. Nothing here obliges you to keep quiet: making it public is also open to you.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    twente.dev is run by one person, so there is no separate committee. That is stated plainly
    rather than dressed up. Reports stay confidential, bounded only by safety or a legal duty —
    never by the interests of a sponsor or an employer.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens to a report</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>We listen, and ask what you want to happen — including "just put it on record".</li>
    <li>
      We act proportionately: a conversation, a warning, removal from the event, or exclusion from
      future ones. Whoever reports never has to handle the confrontation themselves.
    </li>
    <li>We write it down, so patterns become visible even when individual reports look small.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What we act on</h2>
  <p>This gets a response, at any event and in any channel we run:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Harassment, bullying or discrimination, in any form.</li>
    <li>Unwanted sexual attention or unwanted physical contact.</li>
    <li>Persistently disrupting a talk or a conversation.</li>
    <li>
      Recruiting or selling to people who did not ask for it. This is not a job fair; attendees are
      not leads.
    </li>
    <li>Photographing or recording someone who has said they do not want that.</li>
    <li>Ignoring a "no" — to a photo, a follow-up or a sales conversation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Beyond that there is one practical arrangement, which is not a rule but is the intention: the
    default language is Dutch, and it switches to English as soon as someone in the room does not
    speak Dutch. Nobody has to ask for that.
  </p>

  <h2>Photos</h2>
  <p>
    Photography runs on consent. At check-in you can visibly mark that you do not want to be
    photographed, and afterwards one message removes a photo, with no reason required.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this applies</h2>
  <p>
    At every twente.dev event and in every space we run, including this site's repository and any
    future online channels. Speakers, volunteers, partners and sponsors are held to it exactly as
    everyone else is.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
